From: MiaAI_Lab <mia.ailab1@gmail.com>
Subject: glm-cuda: the draft rule's cost seed as GB10 measures it, not sm_120

draft_rule.zig's seed carried sm_120's prices in a comment that says so: 9.4 ms a verify forward, 1.31 ms a row,
1.37 ms a drafter pass. Measured on this Spark pair on 2026-11-03 from the same kind of sweep the comment describes
(587 TF_GLM_DFLASH2_TRACE records, TF_GLM_DFLASH2_FORCE=1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 cycled), a lone verify forward is
38.73 + 5.68 ms a row past the first (r2 0.9983, residuals within +-0.9 ms) and the drafter's pass is 5.92 ms.

What the measurement settled, for the record: the b/a ratio is 0.147 on GB10 against sm_120's 0.139, so a verify row
costs about the same share of a round on both machines. The hoped-for GB10 effect -- a nearly free verify row on a
bandwidth-starved device -- is not there, because a row re-reads the shared dense and attention weights for free but
selects its own top-8 of 288 routed experts, and that expert traffic scales per row. Forcing deeper windows measured
30.7 tok/s against the shipped 37.1 at one stream.

This hunk changes nothing that ships: the default rule is `cut` (cuda_lanes.zig), whose chain-confidence cut never
reads these costs -- Costs.line() is called only from the tpm path. It is here so that anyone who sets
TF_GLM_DRAFT_RULE=tpm on a Spark starts from this machine's prices instead of sm_120's, where a row seeded at
1.31 ms would make the rule offer windows far deeper than GB10 pays for, and so that the next person reading the
file does not inherit a comment that names the wrong GPU. Kept separate from 0096 for exactly that reason: 0096
changes scheduling behaviour and must stay revertible on its own.

diff --git a/zig/src/families/glm/draft_rule.zig b/zig/src/families/glm/draft_rule.zig
--- a/zig/src/families/glm/draft_rule.zig
+++ b/zig/src/families/glm/draft_rule.zig
@@ -175,9 +175,14 @@
     coupled: bool = false, // TF_GLM_DFLASH2_COUPLED=1: a sampled stream's drafts race under its target rows' keyed noise
     copy_skip: bool = true, // TF_GLM_DFLASH2_COPY_SKIP=0 turns it off: no block pass for a stream a copy drafts next
     force: ?[]const u8 = null, // TF_GLM_DFLASH2_FORCE: a window's rows in turn (diagnostic), e.g. "8,1,8,2,..."
-    verify_a: f64 = 9.4, // TF_GLM_VERIFY_MS=a,b,d: the cost seed (measured: sm_120 x8 400 W, 0005-0013, forced widths 1-8)
-    verify_b: f64 = 1.31,
-    draft_ms: f64 = 1.37,
+    // TF_GLM_VERIFY_MS=a,b,d: the cost seed. Measured on two GB10s at TP=2 (EXL3 4 bpw, q8 dense, bf16 head, fp8 KV
+    // on pages, DFlash2 at 4 bits) on 2026-11-03, from 587 trace records over forced widths 1-8: a verify forward is
+    // 38.73 + 5.68 ms a row past the first (r2 0.9983) and the drafter's pass is 5.92 ms. The earlier 9.4/1.31/1.37
+    // was sm_120 x8 400 W, where a row is 14% of a round -- on GB10 it is 15%, so the ratio carried over but the
+    // absolutes did not, and seeding a row at 1.31 ms would make TF_GLM_DRAFT_RULE=tpm offer windows far too deep.
+    verify_a: f64 = 38.73,
+    verify_b: f64 = 5.68,
+    draft_ms: f64 = 5.92,
     lambda: f64 = 0.15, // TF_GLM_DRAFT_LAMBDA: a new stream's tokens a ms before its first rounds
     step: f64 = 0.05, // TF_GLM_DRAFT_LEAN: a stream's logit step a draft
     decay: f64 = 0.998,
